+# Daily Grind origin migration
+
+This static project now builds separate deployments for the existing origin and
+its replacement. No domain or installed-browser profile is available in this
+repository, so no live installation has been moved.
+
+## Configure and build
+
+1. Copy `migration/config.json` to a deployment-specific JSON file and replace the
+   reserved `.example` origins. Both origins must use HTTPS and share a registrable
+   domain (for example, `coffee.example.com` and `app.example.com`). Chrome enforces
+   this using its Public Suffix List; the build does not attempt to approximate it.
+2. Set `oldId` to the existing app's exact manifest ID path. If its manifest omitted
+   `id`, use its original resolved `start_url` path, including any query string.
+   The defaults assume a root installation. Set the install paths to pages that
+   serve the corresponding manifest. Keep existing app names, icons, shortcuts,
+   handlers, and other manifest fields in `scripts/build.mjs` when integrating an
+   existing deployment. The coffee icon supplied here is a fallback asset.
+3. Set `storageKeys` to the exact localStorage keys containing user preferences.
+   The supplied keys are assumptions: the original site contains no settings code.
+   Do not include authentication secrets. Existing destination preferences win.
+4. Run `MIGRATION_CONFIG=/absolute/path/to/config.json npm run build`.
+5. Deploy the **contents** of `dist/old` at the existing origin and `dist/new` at the
+   destination. Include the hidden `.well-known` directory. Publish the new origin
+   first, then update the old origin. Keep the old origin available throughout
+   the rollout; do not redirect its installation page, manifest, or transfer page.
+
+Serve `manifest.webmanifest` as `application/manifest+json`, and the extensionless
+`/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` as `application/json`. The old manifest
+must remain at the URL already used by installed clients; if that URL differs
+from `/manifest.webmanifest`, publish the old manifest there too and retain that
+link on its installation page. Deploy any existing application assets and routes
+alongside these files; this minimal repository only has the home page.
+
+For a Content Security Policy, allow the new origin to frame the old origin and
+allow the old `/migration/source.html` to be framed only by the new origin. Do not
+apply `X-Frame-Options: DENY` or `SAMEORIGIN` to that transfer page. Its message
+handler checks both the exact sender origin and window. Restrictive storage or
+embedding policies can prevent the settings handoff; failure leaves the source
+untouched and retries on the next visit. The destination emits
+`daily-grind:settings-migrated` after importing settings; consumers can reload
+preferences on that event. The source retains its data for rollback.
+
+## What transfers
+
+Supporting browsers handle installed app settings and OS shortcuts through the
+paired `migrate_from` / `migrate_to` manifests and the source origin association.
+Browser confirmation may be required. Other browsers keep their old installation
+and see a link to the new site; that link alone cannot migrate OS integration.
+
+The separate, allowlisted handoff copies localStorage preferences. Cookies,
+IndexedDB, service-worker caches, and origin permissions need their own migration
+or sign-in flow. This project does not access those stores. See
+[Chrome's origin migration guidance](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/pwa/migrate-web-app-origin).
+
+Run `npm test` for manifest generation, failure cases, and settings protocol tests.
+Tests use isolated temporary output and in-memory browser APIs; no server or
+browser is started.
